  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild and showery conditions with overnight fog and low stratus\n   continue through Monday with generally light precipitation \n   amounts.\n\n-  Colder air arrives Tuesday and Wednesday, bringing mountain\n   snow and a mix of rain and snow to valleys.\n\nExpect scattered light showers across western Montana and north \ncentral Idaho through this evening. Precipitation will be light, \nwith snow levels remaining above 5,000 feet, confining any \nsnowfall to higher mountains and passes like Lost Trail. Valley \nfog remains a hazard through Monday morning, particularly along \nand north of Interstate 90 in the Flathead and Mission valleys \nwhere low clouds have persisted. Fog redevelopment in Missoula \ntonight is less certain due to lingering mid- and high-level \nclouds that will limit surface cooling. However, if clearing \ndevelops this evening, fog development will become more likely.\n\nA colder system arrives Tuesday into Wednesday, lowering overnight \nsnow levels to near 3,000 feet. Mountain areas across north \ncentral Idaho, Lemhi County and Southwest Montana, including \nHomestake Pass, Georgetown Lake, Butte, and high passes in Lemhi \nCounty can expect between 2 to 6 inches of snow. Elsewhere, valley\nlocations will primarily experience daytime rain that may \ntransition to light snow overnight. Any minor valley snow \naccumulations will melt during the day but pose a risk of \nrefreezing into patchy ice on bridges and overpasses overnight.\n\nBy Thursday, another low-pressure system dropping south will \nmaintain chances for periodic light showers, though its exact \ntrack and regional impacts are still being monitored.",
